## Core AI Concepts

**AI (Artificial Intelligence)**
The ability of a computer system to learn from past data and errors to make accurate future predictions.

**ML (Machine Learning)**
A data science technique that uses algorithms to create predictive models. Models are "trained" on data, then "validated". Retraining improves accuracy over time.

Training methods:
- Supervised Learning
- Unsupervised Learning
- Reinforcement Learning

**DL (Deep Learning)**
A subfield of ML inspired by the structure of the human brain â€” uses **Artificial Neural Networks** with many layers (hence "deep"). Excels at identifying patterns in images, audio, text.

**NLP (Natural Language Processing)**
Applies AI to help machines understand and process human language as it is written or spoken. Builds on DL principles but is focused specifically on text and language.

## AI Algorithms
Step-by-step instructions given to a computer to solve a problem.

| Type | What it does | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Classification | Groups data into categories | Spam vs inbox emails |
| Regression | Predicts a numeric value | Predicting house prices |
| Clustering | Groups similar data without labels | Customer segmentation |
| Optimization | Finds the best solution | Route planning |

## Generative AI
AI algorithms that generate **new outputs** (text, images, audio, code) based on the data they were trained on.

**LLMs (Large Language Models)**
Advanced AI models trained on massive amounts of text data. They predict the next word in a sequence, enabling:
- Text generation
- Summarization
- Translation
- Code generation

**GPT** (Generative Pre-trained Transformer)
A neural network and ML model inspired by how humans think. Generates content through natural conversational language.

**DALL-E**
An AI model that generates images from text descriptions.

**Whisper**
An automatic speech recognition system â€” converts audio to text.
